{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"adroit-degraded-gourami","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Discovered from\n\nStep 7 sibling sweep during rebar-debug on bug `smart-disloyal-naga` (8386-a512-4815-4e6b).\nSame construct, different entry kind. Confirmed semantically (by reading the code path, not a\nstring match) and then observed at runtime.\n\n## Problem\n\nBug 8386 fixed the write amplification on `tickets-<sha>` entries by hardlinking unchanged blobs\nagainst a verified neighbouring entry. The **code** entry path has exactly the same construct\nand was deliberately left out of that fix's scope.\n\n`materialize` (`src/rebar/_snapshot/repo_snapshot.py`, the `cache.acquire` populate branch)\nbuilds `<store>/<sha>` with the same `_materialize_tree` — `git read-tree` + `git checkout-index\n--all --force` — so every blob is written fresh, with no hardlink and no delta against an\nadjacent entry.\n\n**Observed at runtime** (scratch store, 40-file tree, 4 successive `cache.acquire` calls each\nafter a commit touching ONE file):\n\n    code entries: 4 resolutions -> files=170 distinct_bytes=385118 one_tree~96240 (4.00x)\n\nCharging each `(st_dev, st_ino)` exactly once, four adjacent-SHA resolutions cost **4.00x one\ntree** — identical to the pre-fix tickets number, i.e. zero sharing.\n\n## Why it was scoped out of 8386, and why it is still worth doing\n\nWeaker in the field than the tickets case, on two counts: the code tree is ~41.6 MiB / 3,131\nfiles against the ticket store's ~620 MiB / 69,984 blobs (15x smaller), and the default code ref\n`origin/main` genuinely does have key reuse — it moves per merge, and concurrent gates collapse\nonto one SHA, which is the case ADR 0005 D2 was written for. That is why the tickets key was the\nacute problem and this one is not.\n\nIt is still real: this host holds **29,450 code entries**, and `--ref HEAD` (the documented way\nto review committed-but-unlanded code) makes the key move per commit, which is the fast-moving\nregime the ADR's reuse assumption does not cover.\n\nHeld out of 8386 on blast radius, not on doubt: code snapshots are the **attestation basis for\nsigned verdicts**, so changing how they are built deserves its own review rather than riding\nalong with a ticket-store performance fix.\n\n## What makes this cheap now\n\n8386 landed `src/rebar/_snapshot/delta_tree.py` as a **generic** donor-selection + hardlink-clone\n+ delta-apply helper that takes the source repo, the target sha, and a destination. It was\nwritten not to depend on anything ticket-specific. Wiring the code path through it should be\nsmall; the work is in the review and the test, not the algorithm.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] `materialize`'s populate branch reuses unchanged blobs from a verified neighbouring code\n      entry, or the decision not to is recorded with its reasoning.\n- [ ] A test observes the amplification RED (N adjacent-SHA code resolutions consume ~N trees)\n      and GREEN after (~1 tree), asserting distinct-inode bytes rather than wall-clock.\n- [ ] Each entry still byte-matches the committed tree at its own SHA, and the LFS-pointer and\n      submodule caveat sidecars are still computed correctly for a delta-built entry.\n- [ ] The attestation argument is stated explicitly: sharing storage must not weaken the claim\n      that `<store>/<sha>` is the tree at `<sha>`.","id":"5b25-a463-5038-4c65","parent_id":"","priority":3,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Code snapshot entries have the same no-sharing write amplification as the ticket entries"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787294054182158001,"uuid":"1513c31b-5699-4af5-947f-8fb251b623a7"}